@flexiarg aif/hierarchical-and-temporal-depth
@title Hierarchical and Temporal Depth (Each Level Has Its Own Quantities)
@keywords hierarchy, timescale, strategic loop, tactical loop, temporal discount, calibration state, two-tick campaign, Beta update
@audience aif implementers; anyone coupling a fast loop inside a slow one
@tone technical
@style pattern
@provenance written 2026-08-30 (claude-15) from the catalogue paragraph numbered R15 in p4ng/sec-catalog.tex (PLoP 2026), so that every R-node on Figure 4 has a library pattern; wording follows the paragraph, which is the standard
@see-also aif/temporal-depth-beyond-greedy aif/hierarchical-budget-aware-action-selection
@holds-at R15   ;; pegged 2026-08-30 (claude-15): the catalogue paragraph numbered R15; see PREREG-war-machine §1b

! conclusion: When a fast tactical loop acts inside a slower strategic loop, make the coupling explicit and give each level its own quantities — strategic selection fixes the tactical target, witnessed tactical outcomes update the next strategic calibration state, and longer policies carry a named temporal discount of their own — so that no quantity does duty at two levels at once.

  + context: An AIF harness with two grains of decision: a strategic loop that chooses which task
    to work on and updates priors and preferences slowly, and a tactical loop that constructs and
    executes a cascade against the chosen target quickly. (R13 names the two loops; R11 is the
    orthogonal resource hierarchy over a shared budget. This pattern is the coupling between the
    two timescales.)

  + IF:
      A fast tactical loop acts inside a slower strategic loop.

  + HOWEVER:
      Merely giving both levels a scalar score does not create a hierarchy. A second use of the
      same score — the commitment dial doing duty as a temporal discount, a tactical outcome read
      as if it were strategic evidence — collapses the levels back into one, and nothing in the
      record can then say which level learned what.

  + THEN:
      Make the coupling explicit, in three named seams: (1) strategic selection fixes the tactical
      target — the tactical loop does not choose what to work on; (2) witnessed tactical outcomes
      update the next strategic calibration state — an outcome enters the slow level only through
      a witness the fast level did not manufacture; (3) longer policies use a named temporal
      discount of their own, rather than overloading the commitment dial (R14) to stand in for
      time preference.

  + BECAUSE:
      Hierarchy is a relationship between levels and timescales, not a second use of the same
      score. Each level has its own quantities; the invariant to preserve is that no quantity does
      duty at two levels at once.
      + evidence: the opt-in two-tick campaign records both levels separately, rejects unwitnessed
        learning, folds a grounded fast outcome into a Beta update of the slow state, and uses that
        state to parameterise the next fast rollout. Campaign S (2026-08-21): Tick A's independently
        approved and grounded outcome advanced the slow posterior from Beta(1,1) to Beta(2,1),
        changed the slow mode from exploration to consolidation, and parameterised Tick B under
        that state. This is evidence for the explicit coupling, not for a general nested
        generative model.
      + COUNTERFACTUAL: a run in which a tactical outcome changed the strategic state without a
        witness, or in which the commitment dial and the temporal discount are one number, would
        show the coupling is not explicit.
